│ prophylaxis exists for several populations with advanced or metastatic cancer considered to be at high risk,         │
│ including those with pancreatic cancer, lung cancer, or multiple myeloma. Evidence is inconsistent or lacking for    │
│ lower risk cancer populations, such as those with breast cancer, or for those with malignant glioma, which carries a │
│ high risk for VTE and bleeding relative to the general ambulatory cancer population.CONCLUSIONSUse of antithrombotic │
│ agents has reduced the rate of primary VTE, with minimal increases in bleeding risk in specific ambulatory cancer    │
│ populations. Further investigation is needed to guide and narrow recommendations for primary VTE prophylaxis in      │
│ ambulatory cancer patients.Sage Journals"}, {"study_index": 76, "title": "Prophylaxis against venous thromboembolism │
│ in ambulatory patients with cancer", "abstract": "Patients with cancer are at increased risk for thrombosis, and     │
